  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 6.4. Increased capacity of Member States to strengthen legislation, polices and measures to ensure equal opportunities and treatment in the world of work for persons with disabilities and other persons in vulnerable situations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In August 2022, the Government of Uganda established, and inaugurated in consultation with social partners a Technical Working Group to coordinate operationalization of the HIV&amp;AIDS national policy framework, which was adopted in 2021, contributing to the improved coordination and operationalization of the gender-responsive national policy framework of HIV&amp;AIDS and the world of work.

2.	In January 2023, MGLSD in collaboration with Employers under the Uganda Hotel Owners Association (UHOA) and the Training College (Uganda Hotel Tourism and Training Institute (UHTTI), the MGLSD rolled out the first quality apprenticeship programme in the Hotel Sector, benefiting 95 refugee and host community apprentices who have completed their 12-month apprenticeship training, with 68% of them retained at various workplaces. With the support from the Global Programme on Accelerating Progress on HIV and AIDS and Ending Inequalities in the World of Work, the ILO supported the Government in Uganda to convene a tripartite (plus) stakeholders&#x2019; meeting on the 25th March 2021 to validate and adopt the revised HIV&amp;AIDS national policy framework. The Ministry of Gender Labour and Social Development (MoGLSD) thereafter, constituted a technical working group (TWG), inaugurated on 17th August 2022, to coordinate and oversee the implementation of the HIV&amp;AIDS national policy framework. Through ILO support, the MoGLSD convened a tripartite (plus) workshop on the 19th &amp; 20th July 2023 to consolidate and integrate Tuberculosis (TB) into the revised policy framework 
The ILO supported the Federation of Uganda Employers from 25th &#x2013; 30th May 2022, to mobilise and orient 30 peer educators for the distribution of HIV Self-Test kits among twelve Commercial Agriculture Enterprises, in Luweero, Wakiso and Mukono districts &#x2013; which are characterised by high numbers of migrant young men and women 

The office further supported the Federation of Uganda Employers (FUE) to conduct VCT@work activities to scale up HIV and multi-disease testing in Mukono, Jinja and Mbale districts, which are characterised by migrant young men and women.</narrative>
   </description>
